Create a self-contained prompt that tells another AI coding agent how to build a
Python terminal UI application (Textual-based) for finding and managing
duplicate files on Android/Termux.
Describe purpose, inputs/outputs, dependencies, logic, error handling, edge
cases, and usage.
Do not include code.
Build a terminal user interface (TUI) application in Python for finding and
managing duplicate files on a filesystem.
The script is intended to run under Termux on Android, so its shebang should
point to the Termux Python interpreter path
(/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python).
Write it as a single self-contained module. **Dependencies and imports** - Use
the standard library only, plus the Textual framework (the `textual` package)
for the UI. - Use `from __future__ import annotations` for modern type hint
syntax. - Standard library modules needed: hashlib (for file hashing), sys,
collections.defaultdict, dataclasses (dataclass, field), difflib.SequenceMatcher
(for fuzzy filename similarity), pathlib.Path, and typing.ClassVar. - From
Textual, import: `on` decorator, `App`, `ComposeResult`, `Binding`, containers
`Container`, `Horizontal`, `Vertical`, `ModalScreen`, `Widget`, and widgets
`Button`, `DataTable`, `DirectoryTree`, `Footer`, `Header`, `Input`, `Label`,
`OptionList`, `ProgressBar`, `RadioSet`, `Static`, `Switch`. **Data model** - A
`FileEntry` dataclass holding: a `Path`, an integer `size` in bytes, an optional
`hash` string (default none), and a `filename_normalized` string.
In post-initialization, set `filename_normalized` to the lowercase version of
the file's name. - A `DuplicateGroup` dataclass holding a list of `FileEntry`
objects (default empty list), a `match_score` float (default 100.0), and a
`reason` string (default "exact").
Provide: - `total_size` property: returns 0 if there are no files; otherwise the
size of the first file multiplied by (number of files minus one) â€” i.e., the
reclaimable space if all but one copy are removed. - `size_str` property: the
total size formatted with a human-readable helper. - A helper
`format_size(size_bytes)` that converts a byte count into a human-readable
string by iterating units B, KB, MB, GB, TB, dividing by 1024 until the value is
below 1024 (use one decimal place for non-byte units, and integer display for
bytes), with a sensible fallback to TB for very large values. **Application
behavior** - The main class is a Textual `App` with a header, footer, and a main
layout built from containers, showing a directory selection area (a
`DirectoryTree` so the user can browse and pick a folder to scan), controls for
scan options, a `ProgressBar` for scan progress, a `DataTable` listing duplicate
groups (with columns such as group index, file count, reclaimable size, match
score/reason, and file paths), and a detail/action panel for the currently
selected group. - Keybindings are declared via a `ClassVar` list of `Binding`
objects (e.g., quit, start scan, select/deselect files, delete selected, toggle
options, and similar).
Keep key choices sensible and document them in the footer. - Scan options
exposed in the UI: - Exact duplicate detection via content hashing (MD5 or
SHA-256 via hashlib, reading files in chunks so large files are not loaded whole
into memory). - Optional fuzzy filename matching using `SequenceMatcher` on
normalized (lowercase) filenames, with a similarity threshold (e.g., a
percentage) configurable by the user through an input field; fuzzy groups get a
`match_score` equal to the similarity percentage and `reason` set to something
like "similar name". - Toggle switches for options such as including hidden
files/directories, following symbolic links (default off), and minimum file size
to consider (configurable via an input). - A `RadioSet` to choose the hash
algorithm or the grouping strategy (e.g., size-first prefilter then hash). -
Scanning algorithm: 1.
Walk the chosen directory recursively, skipping unreadable paths and handling
`PermissionError` and `OSError` gracefully (log/skip, do not crash). 2.
Group files by size first; only files sharing a size with at least one other
file are hashed (this is the key optimization). 3.
Within each size group, compute hashes of the files (in chunks) and group by
hash; groups with 2+ members become exact `DuplicateGroup`s with match_score
100. 4.
If fuzzy mode is enabled, compare normalized filenames among files not already
in exact groups (or among all candidates, avoiding duplicating entries) using
`SequenceMatcher` ratio against the threshold; form groups accordingly. 5.
Sort results, e.g., by reclaimable size descending. - Run scanning in a way that
keeps the UI responsive (use a Textual worker or thread, and update the
`ProgressBar` periodically via messages or `call_from_thread`). - Support
cancelling a running scan. - Actions on duplicates: - Within each group, the
user can mark files to delete; by default, keep the first/oldest or first-listed
file and mark the rest as candidates for deletion. - A confirmation
`ModalScreen` must be shown before any deletion, displaying the number of files
and total size to be freed, with Confirm and Cancel buttons. - Deletion uses
`Path.unlink()` wrapped in error handling; report per-file failures (permission
denied, file missing) without aborting the whole batch, and show a summary
afterward. - Never delete the last remaining copy in a group; refuse if the
selection would remove all files. - Optionally support a dry-run mode toggled by
a switch that only reports what would be deleted. - Edge cases to handle: -
Empty directory or no duplicates found: show a clear message in the UI. -
Zero-byte files: either exclude them by default or treat them with the
minimum-size option. - Symbolic links: skip by default to avoid loops; resolve
paths carefully if following is enabled, guarding against cycles. - Files that
disappear or change during scanning: catch errors and skip. - Very large files:
chunked hashing. - Invalid numeric input in input fields (threshold, min size):
validate and fall back to defaults with a notice. - Non-UTF-8 or unusual
filenames: avoid crashing when rendering paths in the table (use safe string
conversion). - Hidden files: skipped unless the option is enabled. - Permission
errors on directories: skip and continue. - Error handling: wrap filesystem
operations in try/except for `OSError`, `PermissionError`, and
`FileNotFoundError`; surface non-fatal problems in a status line or notification
area rather than raising. **Entry point** - Define a `main()` function that
creates the app instance and runs it, and guard execution with `if __name__ ==
"__main__":`.
Optionally accept a starting directory as a command-line argument from
`sys.argv` (default to the current working directory or the Termux home
directory if none is given; validate that the path exists and is a directory,
otherwise print an error message to stderr and exit with a nonzero status). -
Make the script executable and runnable as `python <scriptname>.py [directory]`
in Termux. **Code quality expectations** - Use type hints throughout, docstrings
for public classes and functions, and clear separation between the data model,
scanning logic, and UI classes. - Keep the scanning logic independent of the UI
so it can be tested alone. - Use descriptive names and constants (for chunk
size, default threshold, supported hash algorithms) defined at module level. ---
